  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 207 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nToday winds will be southerly and strong, gusting to 30-40 mph with \nareas along the Buffalo Ridge possibly seeing 45 mph gusts. This is \nin response to an approaching upper wave and surface low pressure \nsystem tightening the SPG. Winds will decrease in the evening, but \nremain gusty as the front comes through. Highs today will be in the \nlow to mid 80s east of I-29, mid to upper 80s west of I-29, and in \nthe 90s west of the James River Valley. Through the day a narrow \nplume of Theta-e will advect in warm and moist air. Instability will \nincrease in concert along and east of the plume. By mid-afternoon \n1500-2500 J/kg of MUCAPE will be available. Steepening mid-\nlevel lapse rates, 30-40 kts deep layer shear, and clock-wise \ndeeply curved hodographs all set the stage for strong to severe \nthunderstorms this afternoon into the evening. This is outlined \nin the SPC Day 1 Marginal (level 1 of 5) Risk. However, \nuncertainty remains leading to lower confidence in this \nforecast. Firstly, we are strongly capped across much of eastern\nSouth Dakota, and western Minnesota and Iowa. Model soundings \nand the 14Z CAMs guidance have begun to increase the amount of \nmoisture in the 850-750 mb layer. Beneath that layer air remains\nvery dry displaying a classic inverted \"V\". From here there are\ntwo possible scenarios. The first scenario is that the cap \ncannot be over come, and elevated storms form along the 850 mb \nfront south of I-90, and east of I-29 after 7 pm. These storms \nmay produce large hail to the size of a ping-pong ball (1.5\"), \ndamaging wind gusts of 70 mph, and brief heavy rain. This is the\nmost likely scenario. The second scenario is the cap is \novercome and isolated surface based supercells form along and \neast of Highway 81 after 5 pm. Surface based storms could \nproduce hail the size of a golf ball (1.75\"), damaging wind \ngusts to 70 mph, and brief heavy rain. In addition, though the \nrisk is low (<2%) a tornado cannot be ruled out. These storms \nwill grow upscale as they move east forming a broken line with \npossible bowing segments. The threats then transition to large \nhail and damaging winds. In either scenario, storms should be \neast of the region by 1 am Saturday. \n\nHighs Saturday will be cooler, in the low to mid 70s thanks to the \nCAA behind the front. Winds will be northerly and light at 5-10 mph. \nScattered light, elevated showers are possible Saturday. These \nshould have very little impact and accumulations will be a trace to \na couple hundredths at best. Modest moisture return in the mid-\nlevels for Sunday will support stratus showers as a low amplitude \nwave passes to the north of the CWA and WAA pushes in from the \nsouth. Rain totals will be modest. LREF guidance indicates a less \nthan 50% chance for 0.25 inches of rain for areas north of Highway \n20 through midnight. In addition to showers, highs for Sunday will \nbe cool, in the 60s.\n\nLate Sunday night into early Monday morning an upper trough over the \nPacific Northwest will begin to move east. A surface lee trough and \nlow pressure will form along the east side of the northern Rockies. \nThis will eject out onto the central Plains and accelerate \nnortheast. A warm front will push out ahead of it with warm, moist \nair pooling behind it. Shortly before sunrise the LLJ ramps up south \nof the front, while at the same time a prefrontal trough takes shape \nand passes through the region. The combination of this forcing and \nwarm moist air riding up over the established cold air over our \nregion will result in more showers and isolated thunderstorms. Long-\nterm guidance is in very low agreement on the convective \nenvironment. While it is possible an isolated stronger to severe \nstorm may form, confidence at this time is low. Highs for Monday \nwill be slightly warmer than Sunday, in the 70s.\n\nBy late Monday evening into early Tuesday morning, the main upper \nwave swings north over the region and the attendant cold front \nwill move through from the northwest to the southeast. This fast\nmoving system brings better chances for strong to severe \nthunderstorms that will pass quickly into central Iowa. An \nunsettled pattern will keep storm chances in our forecast \nthrough the end of the week. Highs during this time will be 5-10\ndegrees cooler than average for this time of year, in the 60s \nto low 70s.",
